# The FineType release this repository stages into its artefacts, and the model
# registry revision the model comes from.
#
# TWO LINES, because the bundle has two sources and only one of them is the
# tag. A FineType release attaches the extension, the taxonomy catalogue and a
# `finetype-model.json` naming the model; it does NOT attach the model weights.
# Those come from the model registry, so a tag alone does not determine the
# bytes a build stages and the revision has to be pinned beside it.
#
# Every consumer reads these through scripts/finetype-pin.sh —
# scripts/fetch-finetype-bundle.sh (which turns them into bytes),
# scripts/package.sh (which checks the bytes it staged carry this version) and
# .github/workflows/release.yml (which names the tag in the run summary).
# scripts/check-finetype-pin.sh runs each of those readers and refuses when any
# of them answers with something else.
#
# WHY A PIN AND NOT A RANGE. Two builds of the same brightfield tag must stage
# the same bytes, and a bump must be a line somebody read. The cost, accepted
# deliberately: this pin can rot silently — brightfield could ship a classifier
# a year behind FineType and nothing here would say so. There is no scheduled
# bump; moving it is a pull request like any other.
#
# BUMPING IS TWO LINES AND THEY MOVE TOGETHER. The tag decides which model NAME
# is staged, because the name is read out of the release's own
# finetype-model.json; the revision decides which registry commit that name is
# resolved at. A tag naming a model the pinned revision does not carry fails as
# a 404 during the fetch, which is the failure direction to want.
FINETYPE_TAG=v0.6.58
FINETYPE_MODEL_REVISION=94cda10a60c3e2eedb831d295c1db091836540c2
